    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **29 tests, 0 failures**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## What I did\n\nAdded a per-city capacity rule to the create-owner pipeline that rejects with **409** when the requested city already contains 50+ owners, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureCityCapacity.java` \u2014 pipeline step that counts existing owners in the request's city (case-insensitive, matching `AssignCustomerCode`'s convention) and throws a checked `CityAtCapacityException` when the count has reached the capacity of 50.\n- `CityAtCapacityException.java` \u2014 checked exception (`extends Exception`) so it surfaces in the `throws` clause and can be routed by an escalation handler.\n- `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT`, mirroring the existing `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler`.\n- `.../escalation/...CityAtCapacityException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Modified:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted a `capacity` step (under `[ transaction ]` governance like its neighbours) between the `household` and `email` steps.\n\nThe step reads the already-validated body via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` (published by `ValidateOwnerFields`), so it doesn't re-bind the request body \u2014 consistent with the single-`@RequestBody` rule. The new `Cp18Tests.coreRejectsWhenCityAtCapacity` (fill a fresh city to exactly 50, expect the 51st to be a 409) passes, and no existing test regressed.",
